     While it's not completely relevant to the Nethack group, I thought I
might comment on Omega:

     I've played a version of PC-Omega (one that actually worked... I
recently d/l'ed another that doesn't).  I actually enjoyed the game for
a while, but I discovered that:
a) it's got more bugs than a beehive
b) the play balance is horrible
     If, for instance, you get an 18 intelligence, either by rolling one
up, playing yourself and being able to answer honestly a few of the questions,
or simply lying (you can create a "super-character" by lying, yes) you can
gain some 5 levels right off the bat (if I remember correctly) by going intoi
the mage-guild and getting a scholarship.
     On the other hand, if you go into the first dungeon without this boon,
unless you have an 18 charisma (at which point you can defeat just about
any monster by simply talking to him, esp. if you decide to be chaotic, q.v.),
you're essentially dead;  the first level contains goblin chieftans at high
frequency (generally along with other monsters) that can easily kill you on a
1-1 basis.  Plus, they can throw missiles at you, so you can't run (missiles
can be thrown without being in-line, so that's no help either).
     Alignment is another big problem; it's easily more beneficial to be
chaotic than lawful.  The only real advantage to being lawful is that you
can join the Paladin's guild at which point you get some reasonably good
items, but nothing particularly good.  The best one would have to be the
Holy Hand Grenade of Antioch, which seems to kill about anything but can only
be used once.
     Being chaotic, on the other hand, lets you join both the thieve's guild
and the sorcerer's guild.  You can also kill of helpless monsters for food
and it's to your benefit; likewise, after T)alking to a monster, you can kill
it if it surrenders.  A free kill, and it's again a benefit to your alignment.
     The god Set will eventually give you the spell Hellfire, a blatantly
unfair tool which lets you kill anything you can see, assuming you can spare
the mana (which generally, by that point, isn't a big problem.)
     Of course, then there's the bug whereby (in the version I played)
you can take something out of your pack and have it go anywhere in your
inventory.  Thus pull a scroll of spells out of your pack into a "ring"
location... you learn a spell.  Lo and behold, when you put it back in
the pack, you not only still have the scroll, but you learn *another* spell.
Since leraning multiple spells cuts the mana cost in half, given enough
patience you can reduce the cost of hellfire to one point.  (Tough, huh?)
     We then come to the "incredible benefit or die" device, which can be
gotten from the archmage if you elect to kill him... you can do it multiple
times, if you please.  Doubles your mana, or kills you.  How does THIS
add to the game?  Either you die or become invincible.  Taking this philosophy
to the extreme, we may as well have the game flip a coin at the beginning
and tell you whether you won or not.  Might reduce the size a bit.
     Even without the scroll of spells bug (it appears the programmer's
normal playing strategy is to pull things out of his pack into the "up in
the air" spot, at which point everything works properly, so as far as I can
tell, he can't figure out where the bug is; at least, that's what I divined
from the update report in the version I found which wouldn't work.  The
bug also causes many problems with two-handed weapons, which you can use to
either duplicate a weapon, or which might accidentally destroy your blessed
+1 lance of kill-anything-evil which you can get, by a different name, after
joining the gladiator's guild, another thing you can only do if chaotic.  To
the game's credit, you can't do this until after you become the champion,
done easily enough with hellfire.  After that, you can get thrown out of the
guild and join the merc's guild and get all of THOSE benefits.)  oh yes,
even without the scroll of spells bug, you can do the same thing with a bit
more effort by wandering about the Archmage's keep; at about level 10, there
is a proliferation of necromancers which, if you can defeat them, ALWAYS
have scrolls of spells.  So just by reading them you can get massive spell
power.
     (Naturally, this takes a lot longer than just switching in-between your
"boots" or "ring" spot in the inventory.)
     Suffice to say that within half an hour, with a little bit of effort and
no risk, you can have about a level 15 character who knows just about all the
spells and doesn't have to worry about mana in casting all but the rarest of
them (the distribution isn't even, so it takes a lot longer to get hellfire
or sanctification [another beaut; creates an altar to your god.  Most monst-
ers that wander by get struck down by lightning] than, say, magic missile).


